    {
      "by": "cubic-dev-ai",
      "ask": "The new sentence overstates the rule. The compile error for a factory returning a storage value applies only to `str`-compatible vars, and backend vars are exempt. Add that qualifier so users do not assume a non-str var with such a factory fails.",
      "addressed": true,
      "path": "docs/vars/base_vars.md",
      "outdated": true
    },
    {
      "by": "cubic-dev-ai",
      "ask": "The claim that this fails to compile \"whichever way the var is annotated\" is too broad. Only `str`-compatible annotations (or storage-typed annotations without a default) raise `VarTypeError`; a var annotated with a non-str type such as `int` or `list[str]` silently gets no browser storage. Narrow the sentence to name the str-compatible case.",
      "addressed": true,
      "path": "docs/api-reference/browser_storage.md",
      "outdated": true
    },
    {
      "by": "cubic-dev-ai",
      "ask": "`_may_hold_str` does not unwrap `Annotated`, so an `Annotated[str, ...]` field can return a storage value from its factory without raising. Resolve the annotation before the union and subclass checks.",
      "addressed": true,
      "path": "reflex/compiler/utils.py",
      "outdated": false
    },
    {
      "by": "cubic-dev-ai",
      "ask": "Handle string-valued `Literal` annotations before these subclass checks; otherwise `Literal[\"light\", \"dark\"]` skips factory rejection and silently drops the returned storage options.",
      "addressed": true,
      "path": "reflex/compiler/utils.py",
      "outdated": false
    },
